From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 23:14:46 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Deliver the Server's channels as descriptors inherited at
 startup

Coworkerd v39, depends on v27 CPU limit events, v30 idle eviction, v32 memory band, v33 memory limits, v35 limit events and v36 watchdog. Fixed upstream v1.20261012.1. A workerd process no longer opens a file of the Server's after startup. Limit event rows, the CPU culprit row and both OOM rows go to the write end of a pipe inherited under COWORKERD_EVENT_FD, each in one unbuffered write() of at most PIPE_BUF bytes: a row is in the pipe when the call returns, and the OOM handler writes rows prepared before entering JS. The culprit row replaces the culprit file; a later terminated row of the same invocation withdraws it, where the file was unlinked. The lifecycle snapshot is published into shared memory inherited under COWORKERD_LIFECYCLE_FD (two buffers under sequence counters, so a reader never sees a torn snapshot and a writer killed mid-write leaves the previous one intact); lifecycle event rows stay on stderr. The memory band is read from shared memory inherited under COWORKERD_MEMORY_BAND_FD. Both are mapped once and their descriptors closed. A dynamic Worker's request context writes its completed row whether or not it ran JavaScript and whether or not a limit is on (without limits only the pipe gets it). The pid is read once per process. Row and snapshot formats are unchanged; COWORKERD_CPU_STATE is gone.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

